      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 (water meters, R49). The certificate number is R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02. Scheme A (indicated by \"-A-\" in the number).\n\nManufacturer: Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\n\nModel number: MS2500 / MV110\n\nThe table describes type details with 3 nominal diameters: 200, 250, 300 (DN). Each DN has 2 columns of values (6 value columns total for each row).\n\nLet me parse the columns:\n\n- Nominal diameter: 200, 250, 300 — with colspan=2 each, so columns 1-2 are DN200, columns 3-4 are DN250, columns 5-6 are DN300.\n\nRows:\n- Q1 [m³/h]: 4.0, 2.5 | 6.4, 4.0 | 6.4, 4.0\n- Q2 [m³/h]: 6.4, 4.0 | 10.2, 6.4 | 10.2, 6.4\n- Q3 [m³/h]: 1000, 1000 | 1600, 1600 | 1600, 1600\n- Q4 [m³/h]: 1250, 1250 | 2000, 2000 | 2000, 2000\n- Q3/Q1: 250, 400 | 250, 400 | 250, 400\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 (all)\n- Q4/Q3: 1.25 (all)\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic water meter\n- Accuracy class: 1, 2 | 1, 2 | 1, 2\n- MPE lower zone: ±3% (Class 1), ±5% (Class 2)\n- MPE upper zone: ±1 (class1) ±2 (class2) % for water ≤ 30°C; ±2 (class1) ±3 (class2) % for water > 30°C\n- Temperature class: For liner PTFE - T90; T50; T30; For liner Rilsan - T50; T30 from DN50; For liner Ebonite - T50; T30 from DN200; For liner polypropylene - T50; T30\n- Water pressure class: MAP16\n- Pressure loss class: Δp40 for each DN\n- Reverse flow: designed to measure\n- Environmental class: O (-25 to +55°C)\n- Electromagnetic environment: E1, E2\n- Mechanical class: B, O\n- Maximum admissible temperature [°C]: For liners PTFE - 90°C; For liner Polypropylen, Rilsan and Ebonite - 50°C\n- Maximum admissible pressure [MPa]: 1.6\n- Orientation limitation: any\n- Indicating range [m³]: 9 999 999 for all\n- Resolution of the indicating device [m³]: 0.01 for all\n- Resolution of device for rapid testing [m³]: -\n- EUT testing requirements: Category: Electromagnetic water meter; Case: Case D\n- Installation details: Connection type (screw thread): Flanged; Minimum straight length of inlet pipe [mm]: 0\n\nPage 11:\n- Minimum straight length of outlet pipe [mm]: 0\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n- Flow conditioner: No\n- Mounting: -\n- Orientation: any\n- Other relevant information: -\n- Length [mm]: 350, 450, 500 (three columns — these correspond to the three DNs: DN200 → 350, DN250 → 450, DN300 → 500)\n- Installation details (electrical): Wiring instructions: -; Mounting arrangement: -; Orientation limitations: -\n- Power supply: Designation of the module for all nominal diameter: MS2500/MV110 HV | MS2500/MV110 LV | MS2500/MV110 LLV\n  - Type (battery, mains AC, mains DC): 100–240 V AC | 24–36V AC/DC | 10–48 V DC\n  - Frequency: 45–66 Hz | 45–66 Hz | -\n  - Internal backup source: 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery | 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery | -\n- Minimum battery life time [years]: -\n- Software version: V.1.04.0005.0004\n- CRC checksum: 9BA34F3B\n- Cut-off: 0,2%\n- Detection empty pipe: on\n\nSo the models here: The model family is MS2500 / MV110. There appear to be multiple configuration axes:\n\n1. Nominal diameter (DN): 200, 250, 300\n2. Q3/Q1 ratio: 250 or 400 (each DN has two variants)\n3. Accuracy class: 1 or 2 (tied to Q3/Q1 ratio apparently — Q3/Q1=250 → class 1, Q3/Q1=400 → class 2)\n4. Power supply module: HV, LV, LLV\n\nSo there are 6 variants (3 DNs × 2 flow ratio classes) plus 3 power supply modules.\n\nHow should I model this? The schema suggests:\n- model_family with models[] — one entry per variant\n- characteristics.type_level — whole-cert attributes\n- characteristics.model_level — attribute × model matrix\n- characteristics.config_level — attribute × config-axis matrix\n- matrix_tables[] — preserve multi-dim tables\n\nThe 6 variants could be labeled like \"DN200 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN200 Q3/Q1=400\", \"DN250 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN250 Q3/Q1=400\", \"DN300 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN300 Q3/Q1=400\".\n\nAlternatively, config_level with axes \"nominal_diameter\" and \"q3_over_q1\".\n\nI think the cleanest approach:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"MS2500 / MV110\"\n- models: 6 variants:\n  - model_id: \"DN200_Q3Q1=250\", label maybe \"MS2500 / MV110 DN200, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\"\n  - etc.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level:\n- nominal_diameter: values per model (200, 200, 250, 250, 300, 300) mm\n- q1: 4.0, 2.5, 6.4, 4.0, 6.4, 4.0 m³/h\n- q2: 6.4, 4.0, 10.2, 6.4, 10.2, 6.4 m³/h\n- q3: 1000, 1000, 1600, 1600, 1600, 1600 m³/h\n- q4: 1250, 1250, 2000, 2000, 2000, 2000 m³/h\n- q3_over_q1: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400\n- accuracy_class: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2\n- pressure_loss_class: Δp40 for all (could be type_level as string \"Δp40\")\n- length: 350 (DN200), 350, 450, 450, 500, 500 mm — wait, the Length row on page 11 has 3 columns: 350, 450, 500. These map to the 3 DNs. So DN200→350, DN250→450, DN300→500. So per model: DN200 variants → 350; DN250 → 450; DN300 → 500.\n\ntype_level characteristics:\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: for class 1 ±3%, class 2 ±5% — this is per accuracy class, which varies by model. Could be config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\". Hmm.\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone: text\n- temperature_class: text\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\" with temperature range -25 to +55 °C\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"B, O\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: PTFE 90°C; others 50°C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range: 9 999 999 m³\n- resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.01 m³\n- eut_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_case: \"Case D\"\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- min_straight_length_inlet: 0 mm\n- min_straight_length_outlet: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- software_version: \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"\n- crc_checksum: \"9BA34F3B\"\n- cut_off: 0.2%\n- detection_empty_pipe: \"on\"\n\nconfig_level for power supply:\n- axis: \"power_supply_module\"\n- attributes:\n  - power_supply_voltage:\n    - HV: 100–240 V AC\n    - LV: 24–36 V AC/DC\n    - LLV: 10–48 V DC\n  - power_supply_frequency:\n    - HV: 45–66 Hz\n    - LV: 45–66 Hz\n    - LLV: null (\"-\")\n  - internal_backup_source:\n    - HV: \"3.7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\"\n    - LV: \"3.7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\"\n    - LLV: null\n\nHmm, but \"24–36V AC/DC\" is a voltage range with type AC/DC. Per rule 3, split into voltage range and type. For config values, the shape is {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Value could be a range {min, max}.\n\nAlso length varies by DN — that's a model attribute.\n\nMPE values: these are complex text. I could record them as strings in type_level or config_level by accuracy class. Let me do config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\":\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: class 1: ±3 %, class 2: ±5 %\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_cold (≤30°C): class 1: ±1%, class 2: ±2%\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_hot (>30°C): class 1: ±2%, class 2: ±3%\n\nHmm, but the fragment includes only the type details table. Since MPE depends on accuracy class and accuracy class is a model attribute, I could also put MPE in model_level. But config_level with axis accuracy_class is cleaner. Actually, the schema says config_level is for \"attribute × config-axis matrix\". Accuracy class is arguably a config axis. Let me use config_level.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider the models. The schema says \"models[]: one entry per variant\". 6 variants of DN × Q3/Q1. The power supply module (HV/LV/LLV) is orthogonal — \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\" says these modules apply for all nominal diameters. So it's a separate axis: power supply module.\n\nSo:\n- models: 6 variants keyed by DN and Q3/Q1\n- config_level axes: \"power_supply_module\" (HV, LV, LLV) and \"accuracy_class\" (1, 2)\n\nmatrix_tables: preserve the main type details table? The instruction says matrix_tables for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Since I'm capturing the data in model_level and config_level, I could include the matrix table too for fidelity. Hmm, but rule says preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The type details table fits into model_level reasonably. I could include the table anyway as a matrix_table for completeness. I'll include one matrix_table for the main flow characteristics table to be safe, mirroring the R137 example. Actually, the R137 example includes both matrix_tables AND model_level. So yes, include both.\n\nLet me construct the matrix table:\n- name: \"Type details by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio\"\n- columns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"q4_over_q3\", \"measuring_principle\", \"accuracy_class\", ...]. Hmm, that gets big. Maybe keep it focused on varying attributes:\n- columns: [\"nominal_diameter_mm\", \"q1_m3_h\", ...]. Actually per example, columns are names and column_units a parallel array.\n\nLet me structure:\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"accuracy_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, null]\nrows (6):\n1. {nominal_diameter: 200, q1: 4.0, q2: 6.4, q3: 1000, q4: 1250, q3_over_q1: 250, accuracy_class: 1, pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"}\n2. {nominal_diameter: 200, q1: 2.5, q2: 4.0, q3: 1000, q4: 1250, q3_over_q1: 400, accuracy_class: 2, pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"}\n3. {nominal_diameter: 250, q1: 6.4, q2: 10.2, q3: 1600, q4: 2000, q3_over_q1: 250, accuracy_class: 1, pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"}\n4. {nominal_diameter: 250, q1: 4.0, q2: 6.4, q3: 1600, q4: 2000, q3_over_q1: 400, accuracy_class: 2, pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"}\n5. {nominal_diameter: 300, q1: 6.4, q2: 10.2, q3: 1600, q4: 2000, q3_over_q1: 250, accuracy_class: 1, pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"}\n6. {nominal_diameter: 300, q1: 4.0, q2: 6.4, q3: 1600, q4: 2000, q3_over_q1: 400, accuracy_class: 2, pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"}\n\nModel IDs: Let me use something like \"DN200-Q3Q1-250\" or \"DN200 Q3/Q1=250\". I'll use \"DN200_Q3Q1=250\" style. Hmm, cleaner: \"DN200 Q3/Q1=250\".\n\nNow the fragment — since this is part 5 of 7 of a single certificate, should I emit the document-level stuff? The instruction says \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nThe certificate number appears: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02. I can include certificate.number and maybe scheme. The scheme is A (from \"-A-\" in the number). Rule: scheme (A|B). I'll include certificate: {number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\", scheme: \"A\"}.\n\nIssuing authority: The stamp says \"Český metrologický institut\" — that's visible in the image marker description. Should I extract from the stamp? It's part of the fragment. Hmm, the stamp description is an artifact note. The issuing authority name can be inferred as Český metrologický institut (CMI). But rule 8 says don't fabricate. The stamp text is present in the markdown as an italic description. I could include issuing_authority.name = \"Český metrologický institut\". That seems reasonable since it's literally in the document (stamp). But there's no address, person, etc. I'll include just the name. Hmm, actually — is the stamp part of the certificate content? Yes, it's on the page. I'll include the name.\n\nActually, let me be careful. The instruction says extract content present in this fragment. The stamp is present. I'll include issuing_authority.name: \"Český metrologický institut\" and oiml_issuer_id maybe null/omitted. Actually, CMI's OIML issuer ID... not stated. Omit.\n\nRecommendation: R49 with edition 2013 (from \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" — the \"2013\" is the R49 edition year, and \"OIML R 49-2:2013\" is cited). The recommendation id canonical: \"R49\", edition: 2013. Accuracy classes: [1, 2]. The fragment cites \"OIML R 49-2:2013, 8.1.8\". The certificate number R49/2013 indicates edition 2013. I'll include recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, accuracy_classes: [1, 2]}. Scheme A — should scheme be in recommendation? The schema shows recommendation has scheme field. From the cert number \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\", scheme = A. I'll include scheme: \"A\" in both certificate and recommendation? The certificate schema has scheme. The recommendation schema also has scheme. I'll include in both.\n\nApplicants: The table says \"Manufacturer: Hemina SPA\". This is a manufacturer row; whether they're also the applicant isn't stated in this fragment. Rule 12 mentions layouts. I'll record manufacturers[]: [{name: \"Hemina SPA\", address_lines: [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana\", \"350 44 Italy\"]}]. Not applicants since not stated.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters; measuring principle \"Electromagnetic water meter\"). Category options in schema list examples: \"Gas meter\" etc. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"] — the model number. Also module designations MS2500/MV110 HV, LV, LLV. The model number is \"MS2500 / MV110\". Type designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"]. Maybe also include \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\" as module variants. Hmm — these are power supply module designations. I could add components[] for the power supply modules? Rule 6: components for complex instruments with role. The power supply modules are part of the meter. I could record:\n  components[]:\n  - role: \"power supply module\", type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\"], alternatives: \"OR\"\n\nThat seems reasonable. And their characteristics go into config_level under axis \"power_supply_module\".\n\n- module_designation: Hmm, for load cell certs this is \"Analog load cell\". Here